1
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:54,000
High seas. Deep terror. Try to stay calm.

2
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:42,000
Dead Calm is a thriller, horror film directed by Phillip Noyce.

3
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:30,000
An Australian couple takes a sailing trip in the Pacific
to get over the recent loss of their son.

4
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:18,000
While on the open sea, they come across a sinking ship with
one survivor who is not at all what he seems.

5
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:06,000
Rae Ingram, played by Nicole Kidman.

6
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:54,000
John Ingram, played by Sam Neill.

7
00:33:36,000 --> 00:33:42,000
Hughie Warriner, played by Billy Zane.

8
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:30,000
Doctor, played by George Shevtsov.

9
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:18,000
Russell Bellows, played by Rod Mullinar.

10
00:48:00,000 --> 00:48:06,000
Danny, played by Joshua Tilden.

11
00:52:48,000 --> 00:52:54,000
Dead Calm is a 1989 Australian psychological thriller film directed by Phillip Noyce,
produced by George Miller, and starring Sam Neill, Nicole Kidman and Billy Zane.

12
00:57:36,000 --> 00:57:42,000
The screenplay by Terry Hayes was based on the 1963
novel of the same name by Charles Williams.

13
01:02:24,000 --> 01:02:30,000
Filmed around the Great Barrier Reef, the plot focuses on a married couple, who, after tragically losing their son, are
spending some time isolated at sea, when they come across a stranger who has abandoned a sinking ship.

14
01:07:12,000 --> 01:07:18,000
Notably, the movie is the first successful film adaptation of the novel, after Orson Welles worked for a number of years to
complete his own film based on it titled The Deep, though it ultimately went unreleased and uncompleted.

15
01:12:00,000 --> 01:12:06,000
Dead Calm was generally well received, with critics praising Neill,
Kidman, and Zane's performances and the oceanic cinematography.

16
01:16:48,000 --> 01:16:54,000
It was nominated in eight categories at the 1989 Australian Film
Institute Awards, including Best Film, and won four.

17
01:21:36,000 --> 01:21:42,000
Modern retrospective analyses have been favorable, with The New York
Times naming it one of the 1000 best films ever made.

18
01:26:24,000 --> 01:26:30,000
The film explores themes of australia, sense of guilt,
toxication, loss of loved one, shipwreck.

19
01:31:12,000 --> 01:31:18,000
The tone is dark and intense.
